Cascade Module (F.L – Forensic Layer)

System‑Level Propagation Model · Lag Detection · Structural Insight

Classification: Internal · Analytical
Parent: Forensic Layer (F.L)
Authority: None (read‑only, pattern extraction)
Status: 🔵 Design – v0.1

---

1. Overview

The Cascade Module formalises the original insight that crypto markets move in waves: a shock in Bitcoin (BTC) propagates to Ethereum (ETH), Solana (SOL), and other assets with measurable lag and decay.

This is not an engine, a signal, or a tactical rule. It is a structural principle of the market. The module detects, measures, and updates these propagation patterns, turning them into a model that higher layers can use.

It sits inside the Forensic Layer (F.L) because it is a form of forensic analysis: observing cause, effect, and timing.

---

2. Placement in the Architecture

```
F.L – Forensic Layer
  ├── Loss Analysis (trade post‑mortems)
  ├── Doubt Mechanism (pattern‑based caution)
  └── Cascade Module (propagation patterns)
```

The Cascade Module outputs patterns that are used by:

· G.O (Grand Orchestrator) – to classify regimes and understand whether a move is local or systemic.
· AUDIT – to verify propagation logic remains valid over time.

It does not influence trades directly. It only extracts and updates the structural model.

---

3. Data Sources

The Cascade Module uses historical price data already stored:

Source Assets
cce_cycles BTC price
cme_cycles SPY (for cross‑market correlation)
data-feed.js internal tickers ETH, SOL, RNDR, FET prices (if stored)
Optional: crypto_cycles table Additional crypto assets

All data is time‑stamped and aligned to a common interval (e.g., daily or 4‑hour).

---

4. Detection Logic

The module runs periodically (e.g., weekly) and performs:

4.1 Cross‑Correlation Analysis

· For each target asset (ETH, SOL, etc.), compute the cross‑correlation between its returns and BTC returns over a range of lags (e.g., 0–30 days).
· Identify the lag that maximises correlation (or minimises prediction error).
· Store the lag and correlation strength.

4.2 Regime‑Aware Segmentation

· Segment data by market regimes (e.g., bull, bear, high volatility) using existing regime labels from G.O (once available).
· Compute separate propagation lags for each regime.

4.3 Confidence Threshold

· Only accept patterns with correlation above a threshold (e.g., 0.6) and sufficient sample size (e.g., >10 occurrences).
· Patterns with low confidence are flagged as “unstable”.

4.4 Decay Factor

· Measure how quickly the correlation declines with lag. This gives a sense of propagation persistence.

---

5. Storage

Table fl_cascade_patterns

Column Type Description
id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY Auto‑increment
source_asset TEXT e.g., BTC
target_asset TEXT e.g., ETH
regime TEXT ALL / BULL / BEAR / VOLATILE
lag_days INTEGER Optimal lag in days
correlation REAL Cross‑correlation at that lag
sample_size INTEGER Number of observations used
first_seen DATETIME When pattern was first detected
last_updated DATETIME Last time pattern was confirmed
confidence TEXT HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW

Optional: fl_cascade_log to record each run’s raw correlation data for debugging.

---

6. Usage by Other Layers

6.1 G.O – Regime Classification

· When a regime shift occurs, G.O checks if the cascade pattern has changed (e.g., lag shortened or lengthened) to infer whether the shift is broad‑based or asset‑specific.
· During high‑confidence cascade patterns, G.O may adjust its confidence that a trend will persist.

6.2 AUDIT – Health Monitoring

· AUDIT checks that cascade patterns are not stale (last_updated < X days) and that confidence is not persistently low.
· Alerts when a previously strong pattern breaks down unexpectedly.

6.3 Dashboard (optional)

· A “Cascade View” could display current propagation lags and how they have evolved over time.

---

7. Reporting

The Cascade Module sends a periodic (e.g., weekly) Telegram summary (prefix [CASCADE]):

```
[CASCADE] Weekly Propagation Update
BTC → ETH: lag 18 days (corr 0.72) – HIGH
BTC → SOL: lag 22 days (corr 0.65) – MEDIUM
BTC → RNDR: lag 25 days (corr 0.48) – LOW
Regime-specific: BULL lag 15 days, BEAR lag 23 days
```

If a pattern has shifted significantly:

```
[CASCADE] Pattern change detected
BTC → ETH lag changed from 17 to 22 days over 4 weeks.
```

---

8. Implementation Phases

Phase 1 – Basic Detection (v0.1)

· Run weekly on existing price data (BTC, ETH, SOL).
· Compute lag using cross‑correlation over the entire history.
· Store patterns in fl_cascade_patterns with regime = 'ALL'.

Phase 2 – Regime Segmentation (v0.2)

· After G.O is running and producing regime labels, re‑compute patterns segmented by regime.
· Update patterns as regimes evolve.

Phase 3 – Integration with G.O (v0.3)

· G.O reads cascade patterns when determining regime stability and trend confidence.

Phase 4 – Monitoring & Alerts (v0.4)

· AUDIT monitors pattern stability.
· Telegram alerts for significant shifts.

---

9. Why It Belongs in F.L

The Cascade Module is a form of forensic analysis. It looks backward at historical data to extract structural relationships. It does not predict; it quantifies what already happened. This is the core function of the Forensic Layer. Placing it here keeps the architecture clean and respects the separation of concerns:

· F.L discovers structural patterns.
· G.O uses those patterns for forward‑looking decisions.
· AUDIT ensures the patterns remain valid.

